# Assessment Core Rules

## Source of truth
- The assessment spec is authoritative: `.cursor/skills/assessment-requirements/reference.md`.
- Never assume, modify, or drop a requirement. If code and spec disagree, the spec wins.
- Use exact names from the spec: endpoint paths, field names (`query_text`,
  `opportunity_score`, `domain_visible`, ...), enum values (`blog_post | landing_page | faq`,
  `high | medium | low`, `visible | not_visible | unknown`), model names, service names.

## Spec gaps
- When the spec is silent or ambiguous, do NOT pick an answer silently. Ask the user,
  using the "Open decisions" table in the `assessment-requirements` skill.
- Every confirmed decision goes into the README "Tradeoffs / Design decisions" section.

## Scope
- Stack is fixed: Flask + SQLAlchemy (SQLite or PostgreSQL) + OpenAI (GPT-4o) or
  Anthropic (Claude) on the backend; Angular v17+ + TypeScript strict + Angular Material
  or TailwindCSS + RxJS + ng2-charts or ngx-charts on the frontend.
- Do not add technologies, layers, or features the spec does not ask for (no auth layer —
  the spec says none is required; no state libraries beyond Signals/BehaviorSubject).
- If a feature seems to need a dependency or infrastructure not named in the spec, ask the
  user first and document the reason in the README.
- "Simplicity and clarity beat over-engineering." Prefer the smallest design that meets
  every requirement.

## Mandatory before bonus
- Complete every Must Have item before any bonus item (async, dark mode, Docker,
  polling, CSV export, skeleton loaders, route animations, tests, Loom).
- Bonus work must never break or replace a Must Have behaviour.

## Data integrity
- Zero hardcoded mock data in the frontend. Every displayed value comes from the Flask API.
- Never commit real API keys. Secrets live in `.env`; only `.env.example` is committed.

## Project layout
- Follow the suggested structure: `backend/` (Flask), `frontend/` (Angular workspace),
  `docker-compose.yml` at the root. Keep file names from the suggested tree.

## Work style
- Implement one scoped step at a time, as agreed with the user; stop when it is done.
- After each step, verify it against the compliance checklist in the
  `assessment-requirements` skill.
